  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \n\nAmid strengthening surface high pressure, dry conditions and below-\nnormal temperatures will be likely thru the weekend. Tonight, \nwill feature the coldest temperature with values in the low 40s \nto upper 30s. With a light breeze expected to persist overnight,\nonly protected valleys have a chance to see calm conditions\nwhich could lead to frost development. Many could see isolated\npatches of frost, however, the most likely area for frost is in\nparts of northern West Virginia and the valleys along the\nAppalachian ridge-line. A Frost Advisory was issued for eastern\nFayette County (PA) as well as Tucker and Preston Counties (WV).\n\nAs high pressure advances eastward, and flow aloft transitions\ntoward westerly/southwesterly, temperature trends seem likely to\nreturn toward normal (maxima in the low 70s and minima in the \nlow 50s).\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nEnsembles continue to trend away from weekend precipitation \nassociated with strengthening coastal low pressure as there is\nmore confidence with its placement. There is currently more \nuncertainty on how quickly the low pressure system will lift out\nof the Mid- Atlantic/New England area.\n\nThe next potential for rain appears to be in the Tue-Wed \ntimeframe, when a trough amplifies across the Upper OH Valley.\nAt this time, buoyancy appears meager, so potential for \nhazardous weather appears low despite a stronger wind field.\nThere is still uncertainty in the strength and speed of this\ntrough, impacting timing and accumulation of the rain.",
